hi this is pressure Walker a group of  people decide to play Secret Santa to  begin each person writes their name on a  piece of paper and places that paper  into a hat each person then takes a name  from the Hat at random and that's the  person to give a gift to the draw is  successful if everyone gets a different  name so your puzzle is what's the  probability of having a successful draw  that is what's the chance that no one  gets his or her own name in the draw  Illustrated each person is getting a  different name but you could imagine  there's a different type of draw where  say person D gets his own name  this is not permissible because you  don't want to be giving a gift to  yourself so this is an unsuccessful draw  so your problem is to figure out what's  the probability of a successful draw and  you need to solve this for a group of n  people give this puzzle a try and when  you're ready keep watching the video for  the solution so first we'll count the  total number of draws that are possible  the first person can draw any of the end  names from the group the second person  can draw any of the remaining names so  since one name has already been taken  there n minus 1 names that are remaining  the third person can draw from any of  the n minus two remaining names that  have not been taken by the first or  second person the very last person will  be stuck with just the one name that's  remaining to count the total number of  draws that are possible we multiply the  number of possibilities that each person  can draw this will be the familiar  formula of n factorial which is the  product from 1 to n now we need to count  the number of draws that are successful  so if you try and count this up like the  first person can select any of the N  minus 1 names but his own name the  second person can select any of the  remaining names hopefully not his own  name and hopefully that first person  hasn't already  selected his name the very last person  is one name remaining and hopefully that  name is not his own so if you try and  count this up it's going to be kind of  complicated so it's going to be  difficult to count so the trick in this  puzzle is you want to count the number  of drawers which are unsuccessful that  is you have at least one person picking  his own name so what we're going to do  is we're going to count the number of  ways at least K people choose their own  names in order to do that we're going to  need the binomial coefficient and choose  K which is a number of ways that you can  choose K items from a group of n how  many ways will at least one person get  their own name well if one person gets  their own name there are n minus 1  arrangements of the remaining people so  that becomes n minus 1 factorial  they're also n choose 1 ways of picking  that one person similarly you can count  the number of ways at least 2 people get  their own name there n choose 2 ways to  pick those 2 people and the remaining n  minus 2 people can be arranged in n  minus 2 factorial ways so this pattern  continues and in general we can say that  at least K people get their own name  we're going to multiply the number of  ways of choosing those K people which is  n choose K times the number of ways to  arrange the remaining possibilities  which is n minus K factorial so when you  simplify this formula you get n  factorial divided by K factorial and  we're going to keep that formula so we  have at least eight at least K people  getting their own name is equal to n  factorial over K factorial so now we  want to know how many of the draws are  unsuccessful so we've been counting at  least K people getting the row name what  we really want is exactly K people  getting their own name so we want to add  up at least exactly one person exactly  two person and so on so how are we going  to do this we're going to avoid double  counting by using the inclusion and  exclusion principle we add up the  numbers where they're odd the No  sets which have an odd number of people  and we subtract out the sets which have  an even number of people so this is the  formula we add up the ones where at  least one people are getting their own  name we subtract out at least two people  then we add back in having at least  three people and so on we subtract out  the number of sets which have at least  an even number and we add in the numbers  which have an odd number so you may  remember this from like a Venn diagram  if you want the probability of A or B  you take the probability of a plus the  probability B and you subtract out the  probability of a and B so when we  substitute our formula in into this  inclusion exclusion principle we end up  with the alternating series of n  factorial divided by the reciprocal of  factorials we can factor out n factorial  and we have the alternating sum of the  reciprocals of factorials so these are  the total number of ways the total  number of draws that are unsuccessful we  can find the probability of an  unsuccessful draw by dividing by the  total number of ways which is n  factorial so that will remove the n  factorial term and so we have the  alternating sum of factorials is equal  to the probability that we have an  unsuccessful draw now to get a  successful draw we take one minus the  number the probability of an  unsuccessful draw so that removes the  first term and we have the alternating  sum of the reciprocals of factorials  starting at 1 over 2 factorial and this  is an interesting sum this is going to  be equal to approximately 37% because  this is the power series for e to the  power of negative 1 or 1 over E so there  are many interesting things about this  one is that we have the constant e which  seems to come out of nowhere the second  thing is we have a 37% chance and that's  going to be fixed pretty much no matter  what size and you have it's  approximately going to be 37%  did you figure it out thanks for  watching this video please subscribe to  my channel I make videos on math and  game theory you can catch me on my blog  mind your decisions which you can follow  on Facebook Google+ and patreon you can  catch me on social media at pressure  Walker and if you liked the video please  check out my books there links in the  video description
 

